5, -0.0401611328125, 0.0318603515625, 0.01349639892578125, -0.01183319091796875, -0.03338623046875, -0.002361297607421875, 0.0343017578125, -0.0009179115295410156, 0.00836181640625, -0.0018367767333984375, -0.0146484375, -0.022369384765625, 0.00838470458984375, -0.0302581787109375, 0.01396942138671875, 0.003276824951171875, -0.0277099609375, 0.010498046875, 0.007602691650390625, -0.00823211669921875, -0.01094818115234375, 0.031097412109375, -0.0386962890625, -0.0022869110107421875, -0.01166534423828125, -0.01473236083984375, -0.006072998046875, 0.00485992431640625, -0.019805908203125, 0.004608154296875, 0.027191162109375, 0.0301055908203125, 0.01125335693359375, -0.01393890380859375, 0.018768310546875, -0.0257568359375, -0.0062713623046875, 0.01312255859375, -0.01001739501953125, -0.004886627197265625, -0.025238037109375, 0.016632080078125, -0.04730224609375, -0.0234375, 0.04815673828125, -0.0185394287109375, 0.00974273681640625, 0.03289794921875, 0.0165557861328125, -0.0135650634765625, 0.006107330322265625, 0.01690673828125, 0.0177154541015625, -0.0304107666015625, -0.0036716461181640625, -0.00910186767578125, 0.0216827392578125, -0.0042724609375, 0.01325225830078125, -0.005886077880859375, 0.01477813720703125, 0.017303466796875, -0.040069580078125, 0.054656982421875, -0.028594970703125, 0.0019369125366210938, 0.01323699951171875, -0.003055572509765625, -0.0189971923828125, 0.01512908935546875, -0.0043487548828125, 0.0259246826171875, -0.0285797119140625, -0.0030269622802734375, 0.009185791015625, -0.0277252197265625, -0.019775390625, 0.01232147216796875, -0.0009059906005859375, -0.038330078125, 0.05047607421875, 0.004680633544921875, 0.014129638671875, 0.0184783935546875, 0.039703369140625, -0.04400634765625, -0.00235748291015625, 0.006809234619140625, 0.017547607421875, 0.01343536376953125, 0.0213165283203125, 0.0020542144775390625, 0.0086517333984375, 0.005046844482421875, 0.0341796875, -0.01238250732421875, -0.0149383544921875, -0.0118865966796875, 0.0239410400390625, -0.007740020751953125, 0.0145111083984375, 0.0229339599609375, -0.003673553466796875, -0.008209228515625, 0.0127716064453125, 0.017303466796875, 0.026641845703125, 0.05352783203125, -0.004680633544921875, 0.01690673828125, -0.01202392578125, -0.0194854736328125, 0.03839111328125, -0.01549530029296875, 0.0237884521484375, 0.0059051513671875, -0.033843994140625, -0.016754150390625, -0.005863189697265625, 0.00785064697265625, -0.00627899169921875, 0.0161590576171875, 0.00586700439453125, -0.0083160400390625, 0.004604339599609375, -0.004825592041015625, -0.005107879638671875, -0.0029850006103515625, 0.012420654296875, -0.01194000244140625, -0.00559234619140625, 0.01300811767578125, -0.0143890380859375, 0.006290435791015625, 0.020904541015625, -0.0135345458984375, 0.0238037109375, -0.028564453125, -0.004543304443359375, 0.031463623046875, -0.0009446144104003906, 0.019775390625, -0.0352783203125, -0.01491546630859375, 0.0009603500366210938], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence (AI) redistributes execution and raises a governance problem: where and how does human direction of the task remain visible and accountable? This issue is practically significant for organizations and policymakers, as the ability to trace and audit human input is essential for ensuring responsibility, compliance, and trust in AI-mediated processes.\n\n**Key Concepts and Definitions**\n\n- **Human direction**: Any human input that shapes, limits, or corrects the AI\u2019s actions.\n- **Governance problem**: Ensuring that human oversight and intent are transparent and traceable in AI-mediated workflows.\n- **Observable traces**: Detectable records of human intervention\u2014such as logs of instructions, edits, or corrections\u2014that are crucial for accountability and transparency. For example, a timestamped instruction or a revision log is an observable trace; without such records, responsibility can be obscured.\n\n**Operational Categories of Human Direction**\n\nWe distinguish two main types of human direction, which differ in timing and form. The following table summarizes the categories, edge cases, and decision rules for classification:\n\n| Category                | Timing of Input | Form of Input                | Edge Cases & Decision Rules                                                                                  | Example                                                                                   |\n|-------------------------|----------------|------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Specified Delegation    | Before AI acts | Explicit instructions, constraints | - If input is before execution and contains explicit, actionable guidance, classify as specified delegation.<br>- Templates: If machine-readable instructions are present, classify as specified delegation; if only implied, depends on AI\u2019s ability to interpret as constraints.<br>- If input is independently auditable as a directive, include. | \"Summarize this report in 200 words, focusing on financial outcomes.\" <br> Selecting a pre-set workflow in an API dashboard. |\n| Iterative Coproduction  | After/during AI output | Review, correction, redirection | - If intervention modifies or redirects output after initial execution and is substantive, classify as iterative coproduction.<br>- Minor edits (e.g., typos) without feedback may be excluded unless they prompt new output.<br>- If input results in a meaningful change or new output, include. | \"Revise this section for clarity.\" <br> Highlighting a paragraph and clicking \"regenerate\" after reviewing output. |\n\n**Classification Decision Tree**\n\nTo clarify classification, the following decision tree applies:\n\n1. Did the human input occur before or after AI execution?\n   - If before: proceed to 2.\n   - If after: proceed to 3.\n2. Was the input explicit and actionable, and independently auditable as a directive?\n   - If yes: Specified Delegation.\n   - If no: Not classified.\n3. Did the input result in a substantive change or new output?\n   - If yes: Iterative Coproduction.\n   - If no: Not classified.\n\n**Direct Contrast**: Specified delegation is proactive and shapes the AI\u2019s initial behavior; iterative coproduction is reactive and modifies outputs after the fact.\n\n---\n\n**Research Question**\n\n**How does the orientation of human-AI interaction\u2014specifically, work-related versus personal use\u2014affect the timing and form of human direction in AI-mediated tasks?**\n\nThis distinction matters because organizations may require robust audit trails for compliance, while personal use may prioritize flexibility or privacy. The operational categories above provide the framework for measuring and comparing human direction across these contexts.\n\n---\n\n**Data and Methodology**\n\n*Data Source and Construction*\n\n- **Data Source**: Aggregate monthly data from the **Anthropic Economic Index (AEI)** for April and May 2026. The AEI is a composite measure constructed from anonymized logs of human-AI interactions across Anthropic\u2019s platforms.\n- **Variables included**: For each interaction, the AEI records user ID (anonymized), timestamp, platform/mode (1P API or Claude.ai), context tag (work or personal), task type (mapped to O*NET categories), instruction content, intervention logs (e.g., edits, feedback), and metadata on task completion.\n- **Nature of AI-mediated tasks**: Measured by mapping user-supplied task descriptions and interaction logs to standardized Level-0 O*NET occupational activities.\n- **Context tag validation**: Tags are user-supplied at the start of each session or inferred from metadata (e.g., organizational account vs. personal account). For analysis, tags were cleaned by removing records with missing or conflicting tags and cross-checking with account type where possible. Based on a random sample of 500 records, inter-rater agreement on tag assignment was 87% (Cohen\u2019s kappa = 0.74), indicating substantial but imperfect reliability. Misclassification may bias results toward underestimating differences between work and personal use.\n\n*Sample Selection and Generalizability*\n\n- **Coverage**: Millions of records from users who consented to logging, filtered for completeness and metadata quality. The dataset spans a diverse user base but is limited to Anthropic\u2019s ecosystem and excludes non-consenting users. This introduces potential selection bias: users who opt in to logging or use Anthropic platforms may differ systematically from other populations. As a result, findings may not generalize to other platforms, non-consenting users, or contexts with different privacy norms or workflows. The platform-specific nature of the AEI means that external validity is limited; caution is warranted in extrapolating results to broader populations or settings.\n- **Classification of Use**: The AEI distinguishes between work-related and personal uses based on user-supplied context tags and interaction metadata. Tags may be inconsistently applied or ambiguous (e.g., a side project tagged as \"work\" or missing tags), introducing potential misclassification.\n- **Modes of Use**:\n  - **1P API**: Direct traffic through Anthropic's API, typically used by organizations in structured workflows.\n  - **Claude.ai**: Aggregates activity from Chat and Cowork interfaces, reflecting more interactive and conversational uses.\n\n*Task Categorization and Inclusion Criteria*\n\n- **Level-0 O*NET tasks**: Standardized occupational activities used to categorize the nature of tasks delegated to AI.\n- **Eligible records**: Data entries with sufficient metadata and clear task categorization in both modes.\n- **Node-month pairs**: Each pair represents a unique combination of a user node (individual or organizational account) and a calendar month. Only node-month pairs observed and eligible in both modes are included (n = 1,411), ensuring comparability but excluding users or periods with single-mode activity. This restriction may introduce bias by omitting users who exclusively use one mode, potentially underrepresenting certain user groups or behaviors (e.g., highly specialized or casual users).\n\n*Simulation Design*\n\n- **Rationale**: To estimate how the profiles of specified delegation and iterative coproduction change under a controlled shift in use orientation, minimizing confounding from other variables.\n- **Procedure**:\n  - For each eligible node-month pair, identify the proportion of tasks tagged as personal and work-related.\n  - Simulate reallocating 10 percentage points of task volume from personal use to work-related use within each node-month pair. The reallocation is performed proportionally across all personal-use tasks, selected at random within the pair, and reclassified as work-related, holding all other variables constant.\n  - After reallocation, recalculate the rates of specified delegation and iterative coproduction for each mode.\n  - Statistical inference: Compute mean differences and 95% confidence intervals using paired t-tests across node-month pairs.\n- **Assumptions and Limitations**: The simulation assumes that tasks shifted from personal to work-related use are otherwise comparable in content, complexity, and user characteristics. This is a strong assumption and may not hold in practice; personal and work tasks could differ systematically in unobserved ways (e.g., in stakes, required precision, or user expertise). If such differences exist, the simulation may over- or underestimate the true effect of use orientation. As a robustness check, the simulation was repeated excluding node-month pairs with high variance in task complexity (proxied by O*NET category diversity), and by randomly reassigning tags to test sensitivity to misclassification. Both checks yielded similar patterns (see Supplementary Table 1), but unmeasured differences may remain. Future research should seek to directly measure and control for these factors.\n\n*Handling of Confounders and Robustness*\n\n- **Observed controls**: The analysis is restricted to node-month pairs with activity in both modes, controlling for user and temporal effects.\n- **Sensitivity analysis**: As above, robustness checks included restricting to more homogeneous task types and varying tag assignments.\n- **Unmeasured confounders**: Variables such as user expertise (e.g., novice vs. expert users), true task complexity (e.g., routine vs. novel tasks), organizational policies, and user motivation could affect both the mode of interaction and the form of human direction. These factors are not directly observed and may bias results if correlated with use orientation or platform. Future work should collect more granular data on user background, task difficulty, and organizational context to better address these gaps.\n\n---\n\n**Results**\n\n| Mode         | Specified Delegation (\u0394) | 95% CI         | Baseline Mean | % Increase | Iterative Coproduction (\u0394) | 95% CI         |\n|--------------|--------------------------|----------------|---------------|------------|----------------------------|----------------|\n| 1P API       | +2.76                    | [2.30, 3.22]   | 18.2          | ~15%       | -0.30                      | [\u20130.55, \u20130.05] |\n| Claude.ai    | +1.45                    | [0.93, 1.97]   | 12.7          | ~11%       | +0.15                      | [\u20130.08, 0.38]  |\n| Difference   | +1.31                    | [0.74, 1."}}